<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ninurta-kudurri-usur (governor of Suhu)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ninurta-kudurri-usur was governor of Suhu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in the 8th century BCE, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamash-resha-usur">Shamash-resha-usur</a>. His long Akkadian inscriptions, found in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anah">Anah</a>, describe building works, irrigation and his defeat of an Aramaean tribal raid on the land. They are a major source for this semi-independent province between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>.</p></div>
